WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.480
Interpreventual transmission bottlenecks continue to constrain clean power additions in Canada despite federal tax credits and coal phase out. This is Environmental Intelligence Episode 73. Global data reports Canada's cumulative installed power capacity expanding at a 2.1% CAGR through 2035, with non-hydro renewables rising from 20.6% to 31.4% of total capacity. The COP-17 biodiversity summit opens in Yerevan

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:34.580
to conduct the first global review of the 2022 Nature Packed, where countries remain

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:40.500
off-track on 22 of 23 targets. Practitioners planning projects in Western provinces

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:46.067
should factor east-west transmission constraints into timelines this week. If you only note

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:51.427
one thing today for your compliance calendar, review inter-provincial grid constraints for any wind,

00:00:51.707 --> 00:00:57.367
solar, or nuclear projects under federal tax credit programs. Power sector consultants and

00:00:57.367 --> 00:01:02.167
remediation planners in Alberta, Saskatchewan, and British Columbia face the main impact.

00:01:03.002 --> 00:01:08.342
Global data's latest Canada Power Market report projects cumulative installed power capacity

00:01:08.342 --> 00:01:14.602
expanding at a compound annual growth rate of 2.1% between 2025 and 2035.

00:01:15.442 --> 00:01:21.282
Non-hydrorenewable capacity, mainly wind and solar PV, is forecast to increase its share

00:01:21.282 --> 00:01:29.822
from 20.6% to 31.4% of total capacity as electricity demand grows at 1.7% CAGR.

00:01:30.402 --> 00:01:35.722
The report identifies inter-provincial transmission bottlenecks as the principal infrastructure constraint.

00:01:36.562 --> 00:01:41.402
surplus low carbon generation in Quebec, Manitoba, and British Columbia cannot readily

00:01:41.402 --> 00:01:44.722
flow to decarbonized systems in Alberta or Saskatchewan.

00:01:45.462 --> 00:01:49.742
Provincial networks remain more extensively connected to U.S. markets than to neighboring

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:56.922
Canadian provinces. Ataraman, Ojindaram, Sybasan, power analyst at Global Data states that Canada

00:01:56.922 --> 00:02:01.562
already operates one of the world's cleanest power systems anchored by vast provincial

00:02:01.562 --> 00:02:07.382
hydropower resources and an established nuclear fleet. The next decade will mark a significant

00:02:07.382 --> 00:02:12.342
evolution with non-hydro renewables driving the majority of net capacity additions,

00:02:12.682 --> 00:02:19.322
led by onshore wind and utility scale solar. Major nuclear life extension programs and small modular

00:02:19.322 --> 00:02:22.422
reactor deployments will help maintain base load stability.

00:02:23.462 --> 00:02:28.362
Practitioners assessing or remediating sites tied to new generation or transmission infrastructure

00:02:28.362 --> 00:02:32.122
should incorporate these coordination delays into project schedules.

00:02:32.682 --> 00:02:38.202
Canada is entering a pivotal phase of power sector expansion as federal tax credits, the phase

00:02:38.202 --> 00:02:43.202
out of coal-fired generation, and accelerating industrial electrification drive substantial

00:02:43.202 --> 00:02:46.002
capital into wind, solar, and nuclear power.

00:02:46.002 --> 00:02:51.002
However, realizing the full potential of these clean additions will require overcoming persistent

00:02:51.002 --> 00:02:55.862
inter-provincial transmission bottlenecks and modernizing regional grids according to

00:02:55.862 --> 00:02:56.402
the analysis.

00:02:57.332 --> 00:03:02.142
Electricity planning remains constitutionally divided across 10 distinct provincial jurisdictions.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:09.600
Almost 200 countries will review progress on the 2022 Nature Pact that set 23 targets including

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.920
conserving 30% of land and oceans.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:18.180
A draft UN review found countries off-track on 22 targets, with stronger progress on

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.520
traditional conservation than on production and consumption changes.

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.680
Environment ministries face funding cuts in both rich and poor countries, creating

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.540
barriers to cross- ministry coordination on root causes of nature loss.

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:36.700
Wildlife populations continue to shrink with monitored populations across nearly 6,000 species

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:44.760
following by an average of 73% between 1970 and 2022, according to the WWF Living Planet report.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.440
Astrid Showmaker, who heads the UN Convention on Biological Diversity,

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.580
notes that progress is moving in the right direction but way too slowly.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:04:00.860
Juliet Landry, a biodiversity researcher at the Paris-Based Think Tank IDDRI, explains that barriers

00:04:00.860 --> 00:04:06.180
include lack of political will for some countries, and lack of access to financial resources

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.440
capacities and technologies for others.

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:15.120
COP17 runs in the Armenian capital Yerevan until 30 October and marks the first global

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:19.480
review of progress since the adoption of the landmark nature pact in 2022.

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:25.060
The agreement set targets from cutting pollution to saving threatened species and conserving

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:27.180
30% of the world's land and oceans.

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:32.700
Progress has been stronger on traditional conservation work than on changing the way goods are produced

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:33.420
and consumed.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:35.980
one of the root causes of nature loss.

00:04:36.954 --> 00:04:38.814
No qualifying industry announcements

00:04:38.814 --> 00:04:41.674
with specific regulatory or compliance implications

00:04:41.674 --> 00:04:43.774
for Canadian environmental professionals

00:04:43.774 --> 00:04:45.054
appeared in today's sources.

00:04:45.694 --> 00:04:47.654
A monitoring data set from last spring

00:04:47.654 --> 00:04:51.014
shows inconsistent dissolved oxygen readings across wells

00:04:51.014 --> 00:04:53.434
screened at similar depths in the same aquifer.

00:04:54.054 --> 00:04:57.214
The variation often traces to differences in purge volume

00:04:57.214 --> 00:04:58.894
or stabilization criteria,

00:04:59.434 --> 00:05:01.194
rather than actual plume movement.

00:05:01.194 --> 00:05:06.734
Experienced assessors cross-check field parameters against historical low-flow records before

00:05:06.734 --> 00:05:08.574
attributing changes to site conditions.

00:05:09.204 --> 00:05:14.034
They also verify that the pump intake sits at the screen midpoint, and that drawdown

00:05:14.034 --> 00:05:16.334
stays below 10% of the screen length.

00:05:16.934 --> 00:05:21.974
The mistake I see most often is accepting stabilization after only three consecutive readings

00:05:21.974 --> 00:05:25.574
without confirming the well has been purged at least three well volumes.

00:05:26.234 --> 00:05:27.434
The fix is straightforward.

00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:32.214
document the actual volume removed and compare it to the calculated well volume

00:05:32.214 --> 00:05:33.994
before recording final parameters.

00:05:34.574 --> 00:05:39.514
Low flow sampling requires careful attention to stabilization parameters, including pH,

00:05:39.774 --> 00:05:42.054
conductivity, temperature, and dissolved oxygen.

00:05:42.754 --> 00:05:47.434
Field teams must ensure the pump rate remains consistent and that the water level drawdown

00:05:47.434 --> 00:05:49.354
does not exceed the recommended threshold.

00:05:50.054 --> 00:05:54.434
Historical data from the same wells provides the baseline for identifying whether current

00:05:54.434 --> 00:05:58.154
readings reflect true aquifer conditions or procedural variations.

00:05:58.774 --> 00:06:03.454
Calibration of field meters before each sampling event reduces the risk of instrument drift

00:06:03.454 --> 00:06:04.414
affecting results.

00:06:05.234 --> 00:06:10.214
Documentation of purge volumes allows later review of whether the well was adequately developed

00:06:10.214 --> 00:06:11.754
prior to parameter recording.

00:06:12.654 --> 00:06:17.394
If you're working on power sector projects in western provinces, review the global data

00:06:17.394 --> 00:06:21.914
Canada Power Market report for implications on transmission-related site assessments.

00:06:21.914 --> 00:06:27.894
Monitor COP-17 outcomes for any biodiversity target updates that could affect species

00:06:27.894 --> 00:06:30.314
at risk acts screening on future projects.

00:06:30.954 --> 00:06:35.174
Update internal project scheduling templates to include inter-provincial grid coordination

00:06:35.174 --> 00:06:37.034
delays for power sector work.

00:06:37.554 --> 00:06:42.914
Mark your calendar for the COP-17 biodiversity summit continuing in Yerevan through October

00:06:42.914 --> 00:06:43.294
30.

